<Docs
  path="/docs/permissions"
  heading="Permissions"
  lead="Coding agents run without approval prompts by default. Only access to credentials and secrets still asks, and Plan keeps an agent read-only."
>
  <h2>The default: no approval prompts</h2>
  <p>
    New coding agents start in <strong>Bypass</strong>. Edits, commands, tests, builds, Git, pushes
    and dev servers just run, so an agent can finish real work without waiting for you. One thing
    still asks: access to credentials and secrets. That request appears in Needs You, and nothing
    is read until you answer it.
  </p>
  <p class="callout">
    <strong>Every permission mode is on every plan.</strong> Plans differ by workflow features and
    allowances, never by safety controls.
  </p>

  <h2>The two starting modes</h2>
  <p>Settings, then Permissions, sets the mode new coding agents start in:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Permission modes" tabindex="0">
    <table class="table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">The modes a new coding agent can start in</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Mode</th>
          <th scope="col">What runs</th>
          <th scope="col">What asks</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Bypass <span class="chip chip--built">Default</span></th>
          <td>Edits, commands, tests, builds, Git, pushes and dev servers, with no approval prompts.</td>
          <td>Access to credentials and secrets.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Plan</th>
          <td>Reading and planning only.</td>
          <td>Anything that changes files, runs commands or reaches out is refused.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Bypass needs no confirmation. Choose Plan when you want agents to read, research and propose
    without changing anything.
  </p>

  <h2>Your provider's own controls</h2>
  <p>
    Every agent is the provider's real terminal, so its own permission system still exists. Claude
    Code, Codex, Cursor and Gemini CLI keep their native tools, settings and slash commands. If
    KalCode is busy, restarting or updating when an agent calls a tool, the call falls back to the
    provider's own permission prompt instead of failing.
  </p>

  <h2>When something asks</h2>
  <p>
    A request for credentials or secrets pauses that agent and shows in Needs You, in the sidebar and on Activity,
    where you deny or approve it. Requests expire when their agent stops or the provider process
    exits; an expired request is shown as expired and cannot be approved.
  </p>

  <h2>KalVoice</h2>
  <p>
    KalVoice works inside the same model. It cannot raise its own authority or approve its own
    requests, and coding tasks it starts keep the provider's native permissions.
  </p>

  <h2>Audit</h2>
  <p>
    Approvals, denials and mode changes are written as events to the local event log, where they
    cannot be edited.
  </p>
</Docs>
